  "children": [
    {
      "id": "GENUS_GAVIA",
      "name": "Gavia",
      "rank": "GENUS",
      "description": "Divers (loons) — ancient, supremely specialized diving birds that are among the most aquatically adapted of all birds. Divers' feet are set so far back on the body that they are barely able to stand upright on land; they come ashore only to nest at the very edge of the water. Their bones are unusually solid compared to most birds, reducing buoyancy and aiding diving — divers can reach depths of 70 metres and hold their breath for several minutes. Sweden holds internationally significant breeding populations of two diver species, the red-throated and black-throated diver, both of which require clean, fish-rich lakes in boreal and sub-Arctic zones free from human disturbance. Their eerie wailing and yodelling calls are among the most primordially atmospheric sounds in Swedish nature. The great northern diver — North America's iconic 'loon' — winters off the Swedish coast in small but regular numbers.",
      "lineage": "Northern Divers",
      "children": [
        {
          "id": "GAVIA_STELLATA",
          "name": "Gavia stellata",
          "rank": "SPECIES",
          "commonName": "Red-throated loon",
          "lineage": "Northern Divers",
          "continents": [
            "Europe",
            "Asia",
            "North America"
          ],
          "subspeciesCount": 0,
          "description": "The red-throated loon or red-throated diver is a migratory aquatic bird found in the Northern Hemisphere. The most widely distributed member of the loon or diver family, it breeds primarily in Arctic regions, and it winters in northern coastal waters. Ranging from 55 to 67 centimetres in length, the red-throated loon is the smallest and lightest of the world's loons.",
          "sourcedFrom": "wikipedia",
